9:     Copy each foreground object anchored in a true grid corner clockwise into the next corner.\n  50: \n  51:     Input structure\n  52:     - The dominant color is the background.\n  53:     - Foreground objects are monochromatic 4-connected components.\n  54:     - Relevant objects are exactly those whose bounding box touches two adjacent borders at one grid corner.\n  55: \n  56:     Rule\n  57:     - Start from the input unchanged.\n  58:     - For each corner object, copy its pixel mask and color to the next corner clockwise: top-left to top-right, top-right to bottom-right, bottom-right to bottom-left, and bottom-left to top-left.\n  59:     - Preserve each object's orientation by aligning its bounding box to the destination corner; stamps may overwrite existing pixels at the destination, but source pixels are not cleared.\n  60: \n  61:     Key insight\n  62:     - Interior and side-only objects are distractors; only components actually anchored to a corner participate in the clockwise perimeter copy.\n  63:     \"\"\"\n  64:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n  65:     assert g.ndim == 2, \"expected a 2D grid\"\n  66:     height, width = map(int, g.shape)\n  67:     bg = get_bg(g)\n  68: \n  69:     by_corner: dict[Corner, dict] = {}\n  70:     for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True):\n  71:         corner = _bbox_corner(comp[\"bbox\"], height, width)\n  72:         if corner is None:\n  73:             continue\n  74:         assert corner not in by_corner, \"expected at most one source object per corner\"\n  75:         by_corner[corner] = comp\n  76: \n  77:     assert by_corner, \"expected at least one corner-anchored object\"\n  78: \n  79:     out = g.copy()\n  80:     planned: dict[tuple[int, int], int] = {}\n  81:     for src_corner, comp in by_corner.items():\n  82:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = comp[\"bbox\"]\n  83:         obj_h = int(r1 - r0 + 1)\n  84:         obj_w = int(c1 - c0 + 1)\n  85:         dst_corner =